    {
      "id": "non-browser-twilite-instructions",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Starting Point",
      "root": false,
      "position": {
        "x": -220,
        "y": 700
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 300,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        },
        {
          "id": "guided-by",
          "key": "guided-by",
          "label": "guided by",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 180,
          "role": "relationship",
          "allowedEdgeTypes": [
            "reference"
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "relationship": true,
            "role": "relationship"
          }
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "non-browser-twilite"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Starting point\n\nMichael: \"I need [a] brainstorm graph for a non-browser based version of Twilite so we can render pages as nodes properly.\"\n\nThis graph exists to start that conversation, not to settle it. The idea, problem, audience, and approach recorded here are a first honest guess at *why* this matters, reasoning forward from that one line — not something Michael has confirmed yet. Treat everything here as a draft to correct, not a decided direction.\n\n## Open, from the very first line\n\n- What exactly breaks about page-nodes today, concretely? (Working guess here: iframe sandboxing and framing policy — worth confirming directly rather than assuming.)\n- What does \"non-browser\" mean in practice — a native desktop app, a different embedding shell inside the existing app, something else entirely?\n- What does \"render pages as nodes properly\" look like once it is working — what can you do with a page-node that you cannot do today?\n\n## Not in scope yet\n\nNo technology choice, no assumptions, no evidence, no next steps — this is the very first pass. Those come once the shape of the idea itself is clearer."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        }
      },
      "handles": [
